<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
=============================================================================
file_paths.xml  —  which of OUR folders the camera app is allowed to write to
=============================================================================

When you tap "Take photo", our app asks the phone's CAMERA app (a separate
app) to save the picture. Normally other apps can't touch our private
storage at all. A "FileProvider" makes a narrow exception: it hands the
camera a temporary address (a content:// Uri) for ONE specific file.

This file lists the ONLY folders a FileProvider may hand out addresses for.
Everything else in the app's storage stays completely private.

  <cache-path>  = a folder inside the app's CACHE storage
                  (/data/data/com.lbunny.roomorganizer/cache/)
     name="camera_photos" = the public nickname used in the address
     path="camera/"       = the real sub-folder: cache/camera/

This matches NodeRepository.newCameraPhotoUri(), which creates the camera's
file in cache/camera/. After the photo is taken, the repository copies it into
the permanent files/photos/ folder, so the cache copy is disposable (Android
may clear the cache when storage is low, which is fine).

The FileProvider itself is switched on in AndroidManifest.xml and points here
with:  android:resource="@xml/file_paths"
=============================================================================
-->
<paths>
    <cache-path
        name="camera_photos"
        path="camera/" />
</paths>
